{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Final Project and Professional Vision","title":"Classroom Implementation Plan","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting classroom implementation plan to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"04","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/06/03"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_calibration","cat531-supplement_06_04_lo_03_direct_check"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_calibration","cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-03":["cat531-supplement_06_04_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_calibration","cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Classroom Implementation Plan\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Classroom Implementation Plan, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-04-classroom-implementation-plan.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Classroom Implementation Plan: Classroom Implementation Plan, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe gap between a good idea and a working classroom is paved with everything the idea forgot: the consent form that never went home, the day the wifi dropped, the student whose device was dead, the success metric no one defined until it was too late to measure. An implementation plan is the document that closes that gap by anticipating failure before it happens. Deciding to adopt a tool is a judgment; making it survive a real week of teaching is engineering, and the two require different work.\n\nA defensible plan does not describe the happy path where everything works. It sequences the prerequisites in the right order, names who owns each step, specifies a fallback for every dependency that can fail, defines success in terms you could actually measure, and states the condition under which you would stop. The Design Tension Studio map records the value decision; the implementation plan is where that decision either holds up or quietly collapses under operational reality.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher plans to launch an AI math-practice tool: \"Week 1, students log in and complete the adaptive module during class.\" It reads clean until a mentor asks three questions. When does parental consent clear, given the tool collects student responses? What happens the day the wifi fails mid-session? What counts as success, and how will you know if a subgroup is falling behind rather than catching up? The original plan answers none of these, which means it is not a plan; it is a hope with a calendar attached.\n\nThe fix is not more enthusiasm but more anticipation. Every dependency the plan relies on (consent, connectivity, devices, a working definition of success) is a place the plan can break, and a real implementation plan treats each one as a failure mode to design around in advance.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA classroom implementation plan is an operational design with five load-bearing components:\n\n- **Gated prerequisites.** Some steps must clear before others can legally or pedagogically begin. Consent must be on file before student data is collected; a plan that schedules the activity in week 2 and consent in week 3 has inverted a gate and exposed the teacher.\n- **Named ownership.** Every step has a person responsible. \"The tech will be ready\" is not a plan; \"the co-teacher confirms the device cart by Monday 8am\" is.\n- **Fallbacks for each dependency.** For every thing that can fail (network, device, login), the plan names what students do instead, so a failure becomes a detour rather than a lost period.\n- **Evaluable success.** \"All students complete the module\" measures compliance, not learning. \"Median student gains one proficiency level and no subgroup falls further behind by the unit test\" measures the thing you actually care about.\n- **A stop condition.** A plan that cannot fail cannot be evaluated. State in advance the signal (for example, a widening subgroup gap at the week-3 check) that would make you pause or revert.\n\nThe course anchors keep the plan honest. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) checks that the activity serves the learning target rather than just keeping students busy on a tool; critical edtech studies forces the fallback design by asking who the happy-path plan would strand; datafication forces the consent gate by asking what student data flows and when. A plan missing a gate, an owner, a fallback, a metric, or a stop condition is not yet enactable, however polished it looks.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A plan schedules the AI activity (which collects student responses) in week 2 and sends parental-consent forms home in week 3. What load-bearing component has failed?\" explanation=\"A gate has been inverted. Consent must clear before student data is collected; scheduling the data-collecting activity before consent exposes the teacher legally and pedagogically. Gated prerequisites must clear before the steps that depend on them.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The success metric is undefined\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A gated prerequisite (consent) is sequenced after the step that needs it\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"There is no named owner\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The stop condition is missing\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nPut the rollout steps in an enactable order so no step runs before its prerequisite has cleared:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Send parental-consent forms home and confirm they clear (data gate)\",\"Verify wifi, devices, and the no-training-data setting; name an owner for each\",\"Introduce the tool to students and run the in-class activity\",\"Provide the printed fallback for any student whose device or connection fails\",\"Check the week-3 subgroup gap against the stop condition\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft often hides the real decision behind a vague improvement claim:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nNotice the shift: the artifact is no longer just polished. The revision points to **critical edtech studies**, names the evidence source, and preserves the reason a suggestion was accepted or rejected.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Rollout Decision\n\nFollow a teacher turning a tool idea into an enactable classroom implementation plan for an AI math-practice tool.\n\n1. **Before (happy path).** Draft plan: \"Week 1, students log in and complete the adaptive module during class.\" A mentor annotates: \"What about consent, devices, and the day the wifi drops?\"\n2. **Sequence the gates.** Reorder: parental-consent forms go out and clear in week 1; the tool is introduced in week 2, after consent is on file. The earlier draft had consent in week 3, after the activity that needed it.\n3. **Plan the failure modes.** Add: if the tool is down, students use the printed practice set; if a student has no device, they pair on a class Chromebook. Owner named for each: teacher confirms wifi, co-teacher manages the device cart.\n4. **Define evaluable success (after).** Replace \"all students complete the module\" with \"median student gains one proficiency level and no subgroup falls further behind by the unit test.\"\n5. **Stop condition.** If the subgroup gap widens at the week-3 check, pause the tool and revert to small-group instruction.\n\nThe before assumes everything works; the after names gates, owners, fallbacks, and a stop condition, so a colleague could run it.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The day the rollout meets reality\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"It is launch day for your AI math-practice tool. Mid-period the school wifi drops and six of your students never had reliable devices to begin with. Your plan said only Week 1, students log in and complete the adaptive module. What do you do right now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Wait for IT to restore wifi and lose the period\",\"next\":\"wait\",\"feedback\":\"A plan with no fallback turns a failure into a lost period. The failure should have been designed around in advance.\"},{\"label\":\"Switch the class to the printed practice set you prepared as the fallback\",\"next\":\"fallback\",\"feedback\":\"You had written the fallback first, so a broken dependency becomes a detour rather than a lost period.\"},{\"label\":\"Assign the module as homework so class is not wasted\",\"next\":\"homework\",\"feedback\":\"This reintroduces the home-access gap for the six device-less students, the exact equity risk your tension map flagged.\"}]},\"wait\":{\"prompt\":\"You lost the period. A mentor asks what you will change. What is the lesson?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Write a named fallback for every dependency before launch\",\"next\":\"fallback\",\"feedback\":\"Right: plan for the failure before you plan for the feature.\"}]},\"homework\":{\"prompt\":\"The six students without home access fall behind, widening the gap. What principle did the homework move violate?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The equity decision in my tension map is meaningless if the rollout strands the students it was meant to protect\",\"next\":\"fallback\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The fallback must keep those students moving in class.\"}]},\"fallback\":{\"prompt\":\"The fallback keeps the class moving. At the week-3 check, the subgroup that used the printed set is now scoring well below the tool group, and the gap is widening. Your plan named a stop condition for exactly this. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep going; stopping now feels like admitting the launch failed\",\"next\":\"sunkcost\",\"feedback\":\"Sunk-cost pressure is precisely why the stop condition was set in advance: to make halting a professional judgment, not a defeat.\"},{\"label\":\"Trigger the stop condition: pause the tool and revert to small-group instruction\",\"next\":\"stop\",\"feedback\":\"You honored the stop condition you set before launch, which is what makes the decision to halt a professional judgment.\"}]},\"sunkcost\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider why you wrote the stop condition before launch in the first place.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"So a widening gap, not my pride, decides whether to continue\",\"next\":\"stop\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Naming the stop signal in advance protects you from sunk-cost reasoning.\"}]},\"stop\":{\"prompt\":\"You pause the tool. What does this episode confirm about a defensible implementation plan?\",\"feedback\":\"A defensible plan survives its own broken parts: it gates consent before data collection, names an owner and a fallback for every dependency, defines success as a measurable learning outcome rather than completion, and sets a stop condition before launch so a widening subgroup gap, not sunk cost, decides whether to continue. A plan you can only run on the happy path is a hope with a calendar attached.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nPlan for the failure before you plan for the feature. For every dependency the activity relies on, write the fallback first; only a plan that survives its own broken parts is enactable. If you cannot state what students do when the tool is down, you do not yet have a plan you can run on a real Tuesday.\n\nCheck any plan against three questions:\n\n1. **Sequence check:** Is every prerequisite (especially consent and access) gated to clear before the step that needs it?\n2. **Fallback check:** Does every dependency that can fail have a named alternative and a named owner?\n3. **Evaluation check:** Is success defined as a measurable learning outcome, and is there a stated stop condition?\n\nIf any answer is missing, the plan describes a hope, not an implementation.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A colleague defines success for an AI rollout as 'all students complete the module by Friday.' Explain why this metric can be fully satisfied while the rollout still fails its real purpose, and write a metric that would catch that failure.\" answer=\"Completion measures compliance, not learning: a class can finish every module while learning nothing, and worse, the class average can satisfy completion while a subgroup the tool was meant to help falls further behind, an equity failure the metric is blind to. A defensible metric names a measurable learning outcome and watches for the subgroup gap, for example: 'The median student gains one proficiency level and no subgroup falls further behind by the unit test,' paired with a stop condition that pauses the tool if the subgroup gap widens at the week-3 check.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan\",\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is the happy-path plan: a clean sequence that assumes consent, connectivity, devices, and engagement all arrive on schedule. Such plans look complete and fail on contact with the first dropped connection. A related error is defining success as completion (\"everyone finished the module\"), which a class can satisfy while learning nothing and while a subgroup quietly falls behind.\n\nThe subtlest misreading is omitting a stop condition. A teacher who has not decided in advance what would make them halt will, under sunk-cost pressure, keep running a tool that is widening a gap because stopping feels like admitting failure. Naming the stop condition before launch is what makes the decision to halt a professional judgment rather than a defeat.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake a plan to use an AI discussion-summarizer in a history seminar. The happy-path draft: \"Students discuss in small groups; the tool summarizes each group's key points for the whole-class debrief.\" Now engineer it. Gate the consent: the tool ingests student speech, so consent and a no-retention setting must clear before any recording. Name owners: the teacher verifies the no-retention setting; a student facilitator in each group confirms recording started.\n\nBuild the fallbacks: if the summarizer fails, each group's recorder reads three bullet points aloud, so the debrief proceeds without the tool. Define success not as \"summaries were generated\" but as \"the whole-class debrief surfaced at least one cross-group connection students had not made in their own groups,\" which is the actual pedagogical purpose. Set the stop condition: if the summaries flatten distinct group arguments into the same generic points (a known failure of summarizers), drop the tool and have groups report directly. The walkthrough turns an appealing idea into something a substitute teacher could run, including on the day the tool misbehaves.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a plan, hunt for its hidden assumptions: every \"students will log in,\" \"the tool will be ready,\" \"all students complete\" conceals a dependency that can fail. Write each assumption in the margin and ask what the plan does when it breaks. The unanswered ones are the plan's real weaknesses. By the end of CAT 531 06.04 you should be able to read any rollout plan and immediately name its weakest dependency and its missing fallback.\n\nA strong reader of an implementation plan can answer four questions:\n\n1. What must be true before each step, and is that prerequisite gated correctly?\n2. For each dependency, what is the named fallback and who owns it?\n3. How is success measured, and would the metric catch a widening subgroup gap?\n4. What signal would make the teacher stop, and was it set before launch?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA plan is strong when a colleague who has never seen your classroom could run it, including on the day a dependency fails, because the gates, owners, fallbacks, metric, and stop condition are all explicit. Formatting is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are the failure-mode design and the evaluable success criterion. If a step lacks a fallback, mark it and ask BigAL to compare a happy-path step with a failure-aware version rather than to \"tighten the timeline.\"\n\nA useful closing line would read: \"I added the printed fallback and the pairing rule because the original plan assumed every student had a working device on every day, and the equity decision in my tension map is meaningless if the rollout quietly strands the students it was meant to protect.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the plan from a one-teacher classroom to a whole-grade rollout coordinated across five teachers. The pedagogical activity may be identical, but the implementation changes shape: ownership now requires a named coordinator rather than \"the teacher,\" consent must be tracked across five rosters, and the stop condition needs a shared trigger so one teacher does not keep running a tool the data says to pause. If you understood implementation as failure-mode engineering, you scale the gates, owners, and fallbacks to the new coordination problem. If you only memorized your single-classroom timeline, the plan will break at exactly the seams between teachers that the original never had to consider.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-04-classroom-implementation-plan-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 06.04. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","stem":"A classroom implementation plan for an AI math-practice tool lists the lesson activities and the login steps. A mentor says it is not yet a defensible plan. What is most clearly missing?","options":["A longer description of the tool's features.","A rollout that names what happens when the tool fails or a student cannot access it, plus who is responsible at each step.","More screenshots of the tool interface.","A statement that the tool is popular with other teachers."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An implementation plan must anticipate failure modes, access gaps, and responsibility, not just describe the happy-path activity.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_happy_path_only","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","stem":"A reviewer annotates a plan: \"You scheduled the AI tool for week 1, but the parental-consent forms are not collected until week 3.\" What kind of error does this expose?","options":["A formatting error in the timeline table.","A sequencing error: a required consent gate is scheduled after the activity that depends on it, so the plan is not yet enactable.","A word-choice problem in the activity description.","A missing citation for the consent policy."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An enactable plan orders dependencies correctly; a consent gate must clear before the activity it protects.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_dependency_ignored","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher asks BigAL to generate a week-by-week rollout schedule for the new tool. What is the defensible boundary on using that draft?","options":["Adopt the schedule as written, since the model balanced the weeks for you.","Use the draft as a checklist, then adjust it against the real school calendar, device-cart availability, and consent timeline the model could not know.","Ask the model to also pick which students get the tool first.","Trust the schedule because it cites instructional-design principles."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can produce a generic schedule, but the teacher must reconcile it with local constraints the model cannot see.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_ai_schedule_authority","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","stem":"Two implementation plans target the same AI tool. Plan A defines success as \"all students complete the module.\" Plan B defines success as \"the median student gains at least one proficiency level and no subgroup falls further behind.\" Why is Plan B more defensible?","options":["Plan B is longer and uses more measurement language.","Plan B ties the tool to a learning outcome and an equity guardrail, so the plan can be evaluated and stopped if it harms a subgroup.","Plan A is wrong because completion never matters.","Plan B will guarantee better results in any classroom."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible plan defines success as a learning outcome with an equity check, making the rollout evaluable and stoppable.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_completion_as_success","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_classroom_implementation_plan","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-01","cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531-supplement_06_04_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-06-04-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_happy_path_only","pattern":"happy_path_implementation","description":"Planning only the intended flow and omitting failure modes, access gaps, and responsibility","trigger":"plan lists activities and logins but no fallback or owner when the tool fails","feedback":"Add what happens when the tool fails or a student cannot access it, and name who is responsible at each step.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_dependency_ignored","pattern":"out_of_order_dependencies","description":"Scheduling an activity before the consent, training, or access gate it depends on","trigger":"a required gate is scheduled after the activity that relies on it","feedback":"Order the timeline so every gate clears before the activity that depends on it.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_ai_schedule_authority","pattern":"ai_schedule_treated_as_final","description":"Adopting an AI-generated rollout schedule without reconciling it to local constraints","trigger":"learner uses the model's schedule without checking the real calendar, devices, or consent timeline","feedback":"Treat the generated schedule as a checklist, then adjust it to the constraints the model could not see.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_4_classroom_implementation_plan_completion_as_success","pattern":"completion_defined_as_success","description":"Defining rollout success as completion rather than as a learning outcome with an equity guardrail","trigger":"success criterion is everyone finishes the module","feedback":"Define success as a learning gain plus a check that no subgroup falls further behind, so the plan is evaluable.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"0b0cec6e58915ec14ae94046fd5427c3b75c1c2c6618c25d94d8209925dca44f","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}